WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.220
An iron core can grow toward the roughly

00:00:02.220 --> 00:00:06.780
one-point-four solar-mass limit for electron degeneracy support. That

00:00:06.780 --> 00:00:09.060
is the core’s mass, not the original mass

00:00:09.060 --> 00:00:11.700
of the whole star. The core cannot restore

00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:15.440
support by ordinary iron fusion. Contraction raises its

00:00:15.440 --> 00:00:18.320
temperature and density, setting up reactions that make

00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:19.520
support fail faster.

00:00:20.200 --> 00:00:23.940
Electrons combine with protons to form neutrons and

00:00:23.940 --> 00:00:27.720
neutrinos. Fewer electrons remain to provide degeneracy pressure.

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:30.440
At the same time, energetic photons break apart

00:00:30.440 --> 00:00:34.420
heavy nuclei and consume energy. Support weakens, contraction

00:00:34.420 --> 00:00:37.360
speeds up, and an Earth-sized core collapses toward

00:00:37.360 --> 00:00:39.840
a neutron-star scale in a fraction of a

00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:40.220
second.

00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:43.440
At nuclear density, the stiffening matter can halt

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:46.060
the inner collapse and launch a shock. The

00:00:46.060 --> 00:00:48.800
shock may stall; neutrino heating can help revive

00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:51.520
it. If the explosion succeeds, the envelope is

00:00:51.520 --> 00:00:54.520
expelled and a compact remnant remains. A neutron

00:00:54.520 --> 00:00:57.260
star has a mass limit too, so continued

00:00:57.260 --> 00:00:59.580
collapse can instead produce a black hole.
